00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.590
Let's now switch to my
computer to have a look

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.680
how this looks like.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.170
Alright, let's jump into it.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.070
So let's start with some basics.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.320
Some basic elements
that should be included

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.377
on every single page.

00:03:18.377 --> 00:03:20.240
So let's start here on the bottom.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.770
What you see here is the date.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:22.603
The current date.

00:03:22.603 --> 00:03:24.588
And pretty frequently
you put here the date

00:03:24.588 --> 00:03:26.780
either when the presentation happened,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:27.842
where you present your pages,

00:03:27.842 --> 00:03:29.851
or when you created a document.

00:03:29.851 --> 00:03:33.130
Because what you will also
find is that many documents

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.149
in professional contexts,

00:03:34.149 --> 00:03:36.021
many presentations, many slides,

00:03:36.021 --> 00:03:39.030
are not actually created
for actual presentation,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.540
but rather as a document
that is then circulated,

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.920
sent around in a company.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.160
And a date is just important,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.426
because when in retrospect,

00:03:45.426 --> 00:03:47.160
you're able to actually know,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.639
to understand when the
document was created.

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:51.908
Which of course, is
important in many ways.

00:03:51.908 --> 00:03:54.945
The second thing that you'll include on

00:03:54.945 --> 00:03:56.210
pretty much every page,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.428
is a page number.

00:03:57.428 --> 00:04:00.120
This of course, is very useful if you sit

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.040
in a meeting and just a colleague wants

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.619
to refer to a specific page.

00:04:03.619 --> 00:04:05.700
Then to be able to say okay,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.480
this is a page 18 and then everybody knows

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.580
what page 18 exactly is,

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.612
because there is a page number
included on the document.

00:04:13.612 --> 00:04:15.610
Then of course, is very important.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.771
Next thing you should
look at is the source.

00:04:17.771 --> 00:04:19.740
So here you can include a source,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.060
such as Firm Learning.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.680
Of course, you can even
then include, just your name

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.060
or your department as a source,

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.680
but even better would be
especially if you refer to a

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.050
specific data, then also
give the source of the data.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.030
So what date are you using?

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.500
Where is the data coming from?

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.970
When was the date collected?

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:39.060
Some specific information
on what data you're using

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.400
is what you can likely
include here in the source.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.600
The next element that you
can use are footnotes.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.290
So, footnotes, one, two, three,
this is just a part where

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.972
you can give specific
details on certain things.

00:04:48.972 --> 00:04:51.380
But maybe just not fit in the

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.040
regular text on your page.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.195
Then you would just
include it in the footnote

00:04:55.195 --> 00:04:56.860
to give you some extra space,

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.900
because it's often a smaller font size

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.070
than the main page.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.870
Just to show additional remarks.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:02.703
And of course,

00:05:02.703 --> 00:05:04.560
one of the most important elements

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.250
of every page is the action title.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.309
The action title is the title of the page.

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:14.240
And creating a good action
title is an art form itself.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.140
Now, I will not go into much of detail

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.236
in this video, what a good
action title looks like,

00:05:18.236 --> 00:05:20.990
because, I already
created a specific video

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:22.950
only focusing on action titles.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.550
The video even includes
an exercise how you can

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.264
improve your action title creation skills.

00:05:28.264 --> 00:05:29.979
If you're interested in that,

00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:31.160
have a look.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.030
I will include a link
to the video above

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:35.740
so that you can directly look into it.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:36.600
So far, so good.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.070
So this was some just really bare basics

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.220
that should be included in every page.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:41.940
Let's jump into something else.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.167
And this is the creation of charts.

00:05:44.167 --> 00:05:45.660
So what you will see in consulting,

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.023
is that almost every slide that you use,

00:05:49.023 --> 00:05:50.770
contains a chart.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.347
So some kind of diagram
where you depict data.

00:05:53.347 --> 00:05:55.176
The reason is that in consulting,

00:05:55.176 --> 00:05:59.530
very frequently, your argumentation
will be based on data.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:01.060
You can just expect from you,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.520
that you will back up your findings

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.723
and your recommendations with
some date-driven analysis.

00:06:05.723 --> 00:06:08.190
And if you do these kind of data analysis,

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.730
of course, you will
frequently use charts to then

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:12.860
show the results of your analysis.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.830
And these are just the
charts that you can create

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:16.370
with the think-cell expansion.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:17.510
If you do not know think-cell,

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.919
it's a plugin that very frequently is used

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:22.357
by consulting firms to create the charts.

00:06:22.357 --> 00:06:24.352
So, almost every consulting firm uses it.

00:06:24.352 --> 00:06:26.120
Of course, if you do not have it,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.020
you can just also work with the regular

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.880
PowerPoint built-in charts.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.720
If you have the opportunity
to use think-cell,

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:32.760
I can highly recommend it,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.620
because it really improves your workflow.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.058
If you now go over this chart tiles,

00:06:36.058 --> 00:06:37.518
many of them you will already know

00:06:37.518 --> 00:06:39.253
and probably frequently use yourself.

00:06:39.253 --> 00:06:42.896
Such as, stacked bar charts,
or the 100% bar charts,

00:06:42.896 --> 00:06:44.190
clustered bar charts, and so on.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.523
Right now, I'd just like
to put your attention to

00:06:46.523 --> 00:06:48.472
the waterfall chart type.

00:06:48.472 --> 00:06:51.020
So the waterfall chart
type is a chart type

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:52.270
that in consulting,

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.774
especially at McKinsey, you
will use all of the time.

00:06:54.774 --> 00:06:56.840
So, there's almost no presentation

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.680
without a waterfall present.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.740
But, outside of consulting,
I really rarely see it.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.010
Just from my observation.

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.250
The fact alone that a
presentation includes a

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.940
waterfall chart, very often
is a clear, telltale sign

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.390
that a MBB consulting firm worked on it.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.360
And actually, most of the
cases, it has been McKinsey,

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:15.540
because, for whatever reason,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.100
waterfall charts are just so commonly used

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:19.440
by McKinsey consultants.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.970
So, let's look at an example.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.700
So, this is just a basic example

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.816
of what a waterfall chart looks like.

00:07:24.816 --> 00:07:27.820
And I'd just like to use
this example to show to you

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.327
what I believe the waterfall
charts can just be so

00:07:30.327 --> 00:07:33.300
helpful in so many business presentations.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.600
So, this of course is
just a stylist chart,

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.200
an illustrative chart, so let's not

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.629
care about the specific numbers.

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:41.120
But, this is now a bridge chart.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.590
Actually, many people,
instead of using waterfalls,

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.130
they also just call them bridges.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:46.040
So, bridge chart.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:47.809
Because, waterfall charts are very helpful

00:07:47.809 --> 00:07:49.870
to show a bridge.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:52.940
To show how one number
relates to another number.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:54.540
One way that this is frequently used

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.870
is to show bridges
between financial figures.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:58.810
So, here we compare the EBITDA.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.310
So, the earnings before
interest text depreciation

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:04.999
amortization of 2019.
To the EBITDA in 2020,

00:08:04.999 --> 00:08:07.208
and we like to understand the difference.

00:08:07.208 --> 00:08:08.992
And if you just look at the numbers,

00:08:08.992 --> 00:08:12.493
here it has been 1.59,
and here it has been 1.67.

00:08:12.493 --> 00:08:15.290
So, at first glance, it
could look like the numbers

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:16.630
are pretty similar.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:17.660
So you could infer,

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.590
that actually the company
has been quite stable,

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.710
there were many things
that actually changed.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.740
If you don't break down
the difference of these

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:26.670
two numbers, into all the effects,

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:28.170
that actually changed,

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.350
from 2019

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.370
to 2020.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.930
You'd see that there are quite

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:34.027
some things going on.

00:08:34.027 --> 00:08:36.670
So here was one effect that if you use

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:38.374
the EBITDA by 700,

00:08:38.374 --> 00:08:41.240
but then this is compensated
by another effect

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:42.590
that increased it by 730.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:44.790
And there's another positive effect,

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.607
further increasing it by 450.

00:08:46.607 --> 00:08:48.047
And a reduction by 650,

00:08:48.047 --> 00:08:50.085
and then another increase in 250.

00:08:50.085 --> 00:08:52.950
So what you can see is
that the waterfall chart

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.744
really enables you to breakdown

00:08:54.744 --> 00:08:57.690
something into the
different sub components

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:58.620
that were driving it.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:00.957
And then enable it to
make changes visible,

00:09:00.957 --> 00:09:03.400
that were just not visible.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:05.120
Just looking at the total difference

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.017
between two numbers.

00:09:06.017 --> 00:09:06.950
Let's now look into another

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:09.192
very ambiguous chart element.

00:09:09.192 --> 00:09:11.513
This is a so-called, callout.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.480
This is this element right here.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.330
So, this is an element that you can just

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:18.000
include in a chart.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.040
Gives details about this specific element.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.720
So as I wrote here, it
can include remarks

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.320
on specific elements of
a chart or other relevant

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.270
contributions that you wanna make.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.420
A callout is something
that you can easily use,

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:30.700
just add details,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.590
add information,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.846
add context to one specific element.

00:09:33.846 --> 00:09:36.030
This is a frequent way how you can do it.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.427
So, this element just
consists of one line,

00:09:38.427 --> 00:09:40.970
and then another line
with a special ending.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.720
So, if you has never
created this yourself.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.629
I just broke down here the elements here,

00:09:44.629 --> 00:09:46.060
you see the line and that this is just

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:49.020
another line, then it's you
can know in the line settings,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.306
you can just add also
such an endpoint to it.

00:09:51.306 --> 00:09:54.050
Then if you combine it,
this is what it looks like.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.503
So, this is just an easy way to create

00:09:55.503 --> 00:09:57.500
this kind of callout elements.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.660
And believe it or not,
but, callouts like this

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.720
will be used in pretty much every single

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.540
consulting presentations that you see.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.170
Another element that
might have catched your

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:07.900
attention is something
that is called a sticker.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:09.921
And this is what you see
here in the top right.

00:10:09.921 --> 00:10:12.810
So, a sticker is a qualification of the

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:13.643
page that you use.

00:10:13.643 --> 00:10:15.840
Here, you see the preliminary sticker.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.380
So, let's now just go over
some examples of stickers

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:20.313
that are frequently and commonly used.

00:10:20.313 --> 00:10:22.602
Then what the context is how you use them,

00:10:22.602 --> 00:10:24.650
in the consulting context.

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.267
So, for discussion sticker,

00:10:26.267 --> 00:10:27.680
the preliminary sticker,

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:28.953
highly preliminary, indicative,

00:10:28.953 --> 00:10:31.690
and then illustrative.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.650
So, these are probably the five

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:35.630
most commonly used stickers.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.710
Of course, you could write
other things as well.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:39.080
And stickers are elements that are

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.169
pretty much used to protect yourself.

00:10:41.169 --> 00:10:43.120
So, why could this be important?

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:44.880
Why would you need to protect yourself?

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.910
So, let's imagine you make an analysis,

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:48.637
which might be a bit controversial,

00:10:48.637 --> 00:10:50.520
you know that their resides,

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.460
will not please everybody.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:53.360
And what you can do is,

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:55.793
to include such a for discussion sticker,

00:10:55.793 --> 00:10:57.870
then if you walk into a meeting,

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.680
then you realize that your analysis sparks

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:01.740
some discussion, and then yet you can say,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:04.457
well, "Yes, of course, this
is not the final result."

00:11:04.457 --> 00:11:06.160
"This is just for discussion."

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.337
As you also wrote here.

00:11:07.337 --> 00:11:08.877
"So let's just have a
discussion about it."

00:11:08.877 --> 00:11:10.987
"I'm interested to hear your opinion."

00:11:10.987 --> 00:11:11.820
"What is your view of it?"

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:13.447
"Why do you believe this is true?"

00:11:13.447 --> 00:11:14.700
"What is your opinion?"

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:15.980
So, this is how you frequently use

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.585
such a for discussion sticker.

00:11:17.585 --> 00:11:20.810
Preliminary and highly preliminary,

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.410
this is just something to make clear,

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:23.697
that the results that you're showing,

00:11:23.697 --> 00:11:25.560
the analysis that you are doing,

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.188
are not the final ones.

00:11:27.188 --> 00:11:30.430
And here again, this just,
is a way to protect yourself,

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:32.360
that then if later it comes out that

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.180
maybe your were missing some data.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:35.640
That there additional information that you

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.769
would need to take into consideration.

00:11:37.769 --> 00:11:40.667
You can just say, "Yes, of
course, this analysis that"

00:11:40.667 --> 00:11:43.027
"we did here was only preliminary."

00:11:43.027 --> 00:11:44.950
"This was not the final version."

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:46.720
This is also again to protect yourself,

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.149
because, otherwise, it could happen,

00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:50.480
and these things happen, right?

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.410
That there is a hostile client,

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.100
that then just points out
a page that you created,

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.030
maybe in the first weeks
of your engagement,

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:58.470
that you were just learning,

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.020
trying to understand the data.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:03.580
And the page you labeled, you
just did, was complete BS,

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:05.411
because this analysis that you created,

00:12:05.411 --> 00:12:07.700
was just obviously not correct.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:08.533
Right?

00:12:08.533 --> 00:12:10.180
And maybe he was right,
maybe it just wasn't correct,

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:12.160
because you didn't have the full picture.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.357
But, then you can say, "Yes,
Mister, this is true, you are"

00:12:15.357 --> 00:12:18.477
"right, but as you can also
read here, this was not a final"

00:12:18.477 --> 00:12:19.647
"version of the analysis."

00:12:19.647 --> 00:12:21.590
"This was just a preliminary version."

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.060
And incidents like that happen.

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.360
And this is why you put the
preliminary sticker on it.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:27.930
That's similar, but different
is the indicative sticker

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.250
and also the illustrative sticker.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.560
So, an indicative sticker,
you can use just to make

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.690
it clear that a certain
analysis that you make,

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:35.484
maybe has some flaws,

00:12:35.484 --> 00:12:37.320
maybe has some drawbacks.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.731
So, the numbers that you show,

00:12:38.731 --> 00:12:41.580
should not be taken that serious.

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.070
But, it gives a good direction,

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:44.440
so that you believe that the analysis,

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.518
the results that you
show, are at least are

00:12:46.518 --> 00:12:48.080
indicatively right,

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:49.590
and these show the right direction,

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:50.850
the right picture,

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.194
even thought the specific
numbers that you use

00:12:53.194 --> 00:12:54.540
might not be accurate.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.249
And, this of course can
happen in any situation.

00:12:56.249 --> 00:12:59.370
You need to work with
the incomplete data sets,

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:00.970
where you're lacking certain information

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:02.730
that you just were not able to use.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:04.530
Then, you might want to
make it clear that the

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:06.720
analysis that you're
showing is just indicative.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:10.740
Illustrative goes kind of
even further than indicative.

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:14.160
And, it's sometimes also
used a bit, in a dirty way,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.380
in the sense that you can
show a whole chart of very

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:20.140
specific information,
very specific graphs,

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:22.510
then you can put an
illustrative sticker on it.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:23.630
You can make it clear that actually

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:25.101
the numbers are not true.

00:13:25.101 --> 00:13:27.850
That you just made up
the whole chart, right?

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:29.830
Maybe, the whole chart doesn't contain

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:32.010
any real numbers at all.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.560
But, you just wanted to
show a certain relationship,

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.160
how you believe that it
is from your experience,

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.900
or maybe from other
information that you have.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:41.125
So, you're just drawing this chart,

00:13:41.125 --> 00:13:44.820
which maybe not even contains
any real numbers at all,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.020
but illustratively, this
is the chart you believe

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:48.853
it looks like.

00:13:48.853 --> 00:13:51.147
So, this is how you put the
illustrative sticker on it.

00:13:51.147 --> 00:13:53.775
So, if you see the illustrative
sticker on a presentation,

00:13:53.775 --> 00:13:56.061
pretty frequently, you
can assume that actually,

00:13:56.061 --> 00:13:57.760
the numbers are not real.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:59.480
The numbers are not actual numbers.

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:01.950
But, this chart pretty
much just was made up

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:03.930
by the consultant who used it.

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:06.620
So, now another element
that you can frequently use,

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.750
is something that is
called a takeaway box.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.100
So, takeaway boxes are something

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.360
like this that you
include below your chart.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:16.268
It includes an important overall remark.

00:14:16.268 --> 00:14:19.060
So, while the callout you saw earlier,

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.390
usually is used for just
given information on

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:23.020
a specific element of the chart.

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:25.130
If you ever have genuine information,

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:26.860
that is relevant to the whole chart,

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.760
you can just include such a takeaway box,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.280
below the chart that you are showing.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:33.200
In the takeaway box, you can
just include the main insight,

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:35.203
the main piece of information,

00:14:35.203 --> 00:14:38.490
that you want the reader,
the viewer, the listener,

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:41.051
to takeaway from the chart
that you are showing.

00:14:41.051 --> 00:14:44.110
Another element that you
can use that looks similar,

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.919
but is often used in a
slightly different way.

00:14:45.919 --> 00:14:48.450
It is something that
you would call a bubble.

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.750
So bubble is sometimes
also called a sticker,

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.647
and it's included in a
chart in some way like that,

00:14:53.647 --> 00:14:56.058
so you purposely make it look like this,

00:14:56.058 --> 00:14:59.010
just included in front of something else.

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.220
It looks a bit less
informal, than for instance,

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:02.170
a takeaway box.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:03.940
And, a bubble you often use to include

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:05.519
some kind of limitation

00:15:05.519 --> 00:15:07.310
to the analysis that you are showing a

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.093
limitation to the data
that you are showing.

00:15:09.093 --> 00:15:11.110
And, a bubble is called a bubble,

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:14.080
because sometimes, instead
of a rectangular form,

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:15.833
you can also use a circular
form of the text that

00:15:15.833 --> 00:15:16.880
you are showing.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.000
So, that the text is included in a circle,

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:20.560
instead of a rectangular form.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.550
From the standpoint,
bubbles are so important,

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.293
you need to put yourself into the shoes

00:15:24.293 --> 00:15:25.740
of a consultant.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:29.120
Because, very frequently, you
receive certain data sets,

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.002
certain informations from your clients.

00:15:31.002 --> 00:15:32.930
And then you take several days,

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:35.250
or maybe even weeks to
then conduct the analysis,

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.309
and then to come up with
some kind of a result.

00:15:37.309 --> 00:15:39.510
Then very often, what
happens, is that then,

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.476
the last days, some anxious hours before

00:15:41.476 --> 00:15:43.160
the big presentation,

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:44.840
where you then want to show the results,

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.517
you receive some new information,

00:15:46.517 --> 00:15:49.550
maybe you realize that now
some new data came out,

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:51.750
maybe now, the next month's finished,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:53.990
and now you put, re-do all the analysis,

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.460
with all the data from
the new time period,

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:57.380
from the new month.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:00.110
In these situations, you're
very frequently faced with

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:02.860
the decision to whether
you want to re-do now,

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:03.910
all the analysis,

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:06.117
to incorporate this new
information, or whether,

00:16:06.117 --> 00:16:08.363
will you leave all the analysis as-is?

00:16:08.363 --> 00:16:11.630
And, then just include
a bubble on the page,

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:14.620
stating the limitation,
or stating what has not

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:17.170
yet been included in the analysis.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.304
Bubbles are so important,
because very frequently,

00:16:19.304 --> 00:16:22.990
instead of re-doing the whole
thing, re-doing analysis,

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:24.760
re-creating all kinds of pages.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.830
Problems like that, you can
frequently use with the bubble.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:31.180
And, this is why there is a
magic saying in consulting,

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:34.276
and this goes like, "If
in trouble use a bubble!"

00:16:34.276 --> 00:16:36.126
So, if you are struggling with something,

00:16:36.126 --> 00:16:38.310
something just isn't right.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:40.290
You just need to use this statement,

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.080
just need to make some corrections,

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:43.527
in the end, if you're in trouble,

00:16:43.527 --> 00:16:46.590
most of the problems you
can just use with a bubble.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:49.500
Just stating the specific
things that you want to change.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.880
Now, some other frequently used elements

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.332
are circles with numbers.

00:16:54.332 --> 00:16:56.781
This, again, is something
that I very frequently

00:16:56.781 --> 00:16:58.810
see in consulting presentations,

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:03.180
but I rarely see lines of
circles with numbers like that in

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.954
presentations that are being
done by non-consulting people.

00:17:05.954 --> 00:17:08.530
So, how do you use these things.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.068
So, you can use these kinds
of number circles, whenever,

00:17:11.068 --> 00:17:14.470
you want to include another layer of data.

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:16.630
Another dimension of numbers,

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:19.170
which is not directly part of the chart.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:21.240
So, let's look at this stylized chart.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:22.240
So, what do we see here?

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:24.940
So, these are the revenue
figures of a business.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:26.330
But, not the absolute figures,

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:29.010
but the difference to the previous year.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.650
So, we look into these calendar weeks,

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.280
and we look into how
the business performed

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:34.640
against the previous years.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:36.780
So, here, so this would
mean that in the calendar

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:38.290
week seven of the year,

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:41.947
the business had revenues that
were by a 100 million higher

00:17:41.947 --> 00:17:43.590
than in the previous year.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:46.990
You have 200 million
higher, 250 million higher,

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:49.200
here a 100 million less, and so on.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.090
Of course, in this context,
if you look at this,

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:53.350
it would be interesting
and important to us,

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:55.570
to include what the absolute amount,

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:58.340
what the revenue was in
the current year 2020.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:01.170
In situations like that, where
you just want to include as

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:04.680
a second point of data,
second point of information,

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:08.390
circles of numbers like that
are just very commonly used.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:10.880
So, here you would now
include information,

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:12.700
that in the calendar week seven,

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:16.160
you make now the revenues of 900 million,

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:18.320
and this was 100 million more

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:20.180
than you made in the previous year.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.083
Then, regarding the
legend, what you do is,

00:18:22.083 --> 00:18:25.790
the main legend that you
state in the chart title,

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:27.810
the main legend always refers to the

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:29.400
numbers in the main chart.

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:32.363
And, for the circles, you
can include a sub-legend,

00:18:32.363 --> 00:18:36.770
then explain what is shown
and depicted in these circles.

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:39.480
And, if you're interested to
learn more about how to set up

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.620
consulting style charts, like that.

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:44.500
I created a whole other
video just discussing

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:46.250
how you should set up the chart title,

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:48.630
then also the subtitle,
and things like that.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.320
So, if you haven't seen that yet,

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.010
I believe this is also highly relevant,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:53.830
and something that you need to know,

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:55.050
if you work in consulting,

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:56.920
or want to create
presentations that meet the

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:58.465
standards of consulting firms.

00:18:58.465 --> 00:19:00.630
I will leave it above here for you,

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.770
check it out if you'd
like to see it as well.

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:05.160
Next concept I would like
to talk about is also a

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:07.230
very, very important one.

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:09.920
And, it's the concept of clotheslines.

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:13.190
More specifically, of
avoiding clotheslines.

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:16.380
So, what do we call
clotheslines in consulting?

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:21.200
Clotheslines are referred to
as just long lists of things,

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:22.850
and specifically in the context

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:25.020
of creating PowerPoint presentations,

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.250
just long lists of bullets.

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.690
So, what you will always never see in

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:31.171
consulting style presentations,

00:19:31.171 --> 00:19:33.507
is just the list of bullets like that,

00:19:33.507 --> 00:19:36.780
where you just randomly
list all kinds of thoughts,

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:38.950
or other explanations that you want to.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:40.570
So, why is this bad?

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:42.948
So, if you just use a list
of thoughts like this,

00:19:42.948 --> 00:19:45.450
what you're missing,
what you're not showing,

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:49.410
what you're not doing, is giving
it some kind of structure.

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:50.815
If you present things like that,

00:19:50.815 --> 00:19:53.060
it looks completely random.

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:54.190
The person looking at it,

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:57.210
can have no trust at all
that actually the things

00:19:57.210 --> 00:19:59.010
that are mentioned here are complete,

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:00.360
that all the relevant aspects

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:02.095
have been discussed and addressed by you.

00:20:02.095 --> 00:20:04.780
But, it just seems like
a completely random

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:07.740
unstructured list of points.

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.900
In such an unstructured list of points,

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.900
is what you would call a clothesline.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:13.970
And, you want to avoid clotheslines.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:16.410
So, what is now used
in consulting to avoid

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:17.920
clotheslines like that?

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:19.180
And, very frequently,

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:21.930
what you do is to use structure elements.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:23.835
So, here again, just in
the illustrative page,

00:20:23.835 --> 00:20:27.050
this could be out of, for
instance, an M&amp;A transaction,

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:28.542
a merger's and acquisition transaction.

00:20:28.542 --> 00:20:31.523
Where one company aims
to buy another company.

00:20:31.523 --> 00:20:35.056
Another company is showing
how the purchasing price

00:20:35.056 --> 00:20:38.260
that they want to offer
for a company splits down

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:40.660
to the different assets
that the company has,

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:42.140
and that the company is selling.

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:43.230
Here again, this is now,

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.174
another frequent use of a waterfall chart,

00:20:45.174 --> 00:20:47.753
that you now have the
total purchasing price.

00:20:47.753 --> 00:20:51.210
Now you show how this breaks
down into the different

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.460
subelements of the company.

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:55.790
Now, here you have the different
assets that the company

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:58.650
has to offer us, so here,
property, plant and equipment.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:00.760
Then, of course, there could
be a whole lot of other

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:02.200
assets that the company offers,

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:04.060
for instance, inventory, and so on.

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:07.280
Now, a clothesline would be
just to state all these assets,

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:09.730
just in a random list of bullets.

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:11.440
Now, to avoid clotheslines,

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.610
what you often use is structure elements.

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:16.691
So, what you try, is to define buckets,

00:21:16.691 --> 00:21:19.183
to define groups of elements,

00:21:19.183 --> 00:21:23.373
then you can use to organize
the different subelements,

00:21:23.373 --> 00:21:25.271
that are from bullet points that you have.

00:21:25.271 --> 00:21:28.772
Now, here just a simple
example is to use gray bars,

00:21:28.772 --> 00:21:31.160
that you include in front of the elements,

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:33.911
then the company you will
often distinguish the

00:21:33.911 --> 00:21:36.092
fixed assets from the current assets.

00:21:36.092 --> 00:21:38.129
This is now a structure that I defined,

00:21:38.129 --> 00:21:39.604
in that, in this structure,

00:21:39.604 --> 00:21:42.014
now I can use a different bullet points.

00:21:42.014 --> 00:21:44.040
And, I hope you now understand,
that this is superior

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.880
to other ways of structuring the points.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:47.830
Because, now, by having
to use the structure,

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:49.340
fixed assets, and current assets,

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.560
I already have some kind of guarantee,

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:55.120
that if I now look at
these two types of assets,

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:56.722
I will catch all the assets

00:21:56.722 --> 00:21:59.380
that are included in the company.

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:01.110
Because, this way, now I have covered

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:02.840
the whole balance sheet of the company.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:06.090
And, I can be sure that
I will have covered all

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.360
the assets within these two groups.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:09.954
So, always use structure elements,

00:22:09.954 --> 00:22:13.160
avoid at all costs, using clotheslines,

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:14.960
because, this is just
completely unprofessional.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:17.340
This is probably one of the
first points of feedback

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.210
that you will get as a young consultant.

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:21.320
If you created a page just using

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:22.910
a list of bullet points like this.

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:25.155
